<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Sending Apple Events to another application is blocked under the
         hardened runtime unless this entitlement is present. ProcessX needs it
         for one feature: asking a browser for its open tabs by name, and
         switching to one (BrowserTabs.swift).

         Without it the failure is actively misleading rather than merely
         missing. A blocked Apple Event returns -1743, errAEEventNotPermitted —
         the identical code macOS returns when the user has denied Automation
         permission. BrowserTabs maps -1743 to "not-permitted" and the row
         offers "Allow ProcessX to control Chrome... Open Settings", so the user
         is sent to grant a permission they may already have, and it still
         fails. The self-test asserts this entitlement is present whenever the
         hardened runtime flag is set, because the two must ship together.

         Nothing else is claimed. No JIT, no unsigned executable memory, no
         disabled library validation: ProcessX loads no plug-ins and no
         third-party code, and every hardened-runtime exception is attack
         surface that has to be justified rather than copied in. Full Disk
         Access and Automation are TCC grants the user makes, not entitlements.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
